        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Mir 22 crew, Commander Valeri Korzun and Flight Engineer Aleksandr Kaleri, launched in a Soyuz TM-24 spacecraft from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan on August 17 to replace the Russian Mir 21 crew on board the Mir Space Station.  Included in the Soyuz TM-24 crew was the first female French cosmonaut, Claudie Deshays, who stayed aboard the station for 16 days and returned to Earth with the Mir 21 crew on September 2, 1996.<P>During the Mir 22 mission, three Shuttle dockings occurred:  STS-79, STS-81, and STS-84.  STS-79 brought John Blaha, the NASA 3 crewmember, to replace Shannon Lucid, the NASA 2 crewmember.   During the five days of joint operations, astronauts and cosmonauts transferred science samples and data collected by the Mir 21/NASA 2 crew from Mir to the Shuttle.  Crewmembers also transferred hardware and supplies to Mir in support of the health and well being of the Mir 22/NASA 3 crew, as well as to support future investigations on the station. Many of the resupply items were used directly for scientific investigations performed during the NASA 3 mission.  The NASA 3 crewmember remained on board the station for 118 days and returned to Earth on the Shuttle with the STS-81 crew.   STS-81 also brought the NASA 4 crewmember to the Mir Space Station. The NASA 4 crewmember remained on board the station for 123 days, and returned to Earth with the STS-84 crew.<P>Two extravehicular activities (EVAs) were performed during the Mir 22 mission.  The main objectives of the EVAs were to attach power cables to a new solar array and to mount an antenna that would facilitate docking of the Soyuz and Progress spacecraft to the station.<P>Science conducted on Mir during the long-duration missions was diverse.  Research areas included human life sciences, fundamental biology to study animals and plants, biotechnology, materials sciences, and spacecraft structural dynamics and environment. Results assisted researchers in developing the operating procedures on board the International Space Station (ISS).<P>On February 12, 1997, the Russian Mir 23 crew arrived, joining the NASA 4 crewmember on Mir and replacing the Mir 22 crew.<END>
        </div>

